.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-CREATE-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.33" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-create-application \- define an application from an artifact in a bucket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap create-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-runtime | \-r
.I RUNTIME
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I BUCKET
.BR \-\-artifact | \-a
.I KEY
[\fB\-\-user\fR|\fB\-u\fR \fIUSER\fR]
[\fB\-\-buckets\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-queues\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-command\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fICMD\fR]
[\fB\-\-arguments\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-environment\fR|\fB\-e\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-min\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-ready\-timeout\fR \fIMS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Defines a new application from an artifact already stored in an
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
bucket - upload it first with
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1),
or through a transfer server.
.PP
The manager copies the artifact to the host, starts it with the
runtime's interpreter (or directly, for
.IR BINARY ),
and scales it between
.B \-\-min\-instances
and
.B \-\-max\-instances
like any other module: round-robin across instances, another instance
when they are all busy, one fewer after a spell of idleness.
.PP
The bucket, the artifact and (when given) the user are resolved now
rather than at start-up, so a typo is reported here instead of surfacing
later as an application that never comes up.
.PP
The application is created
.IR stopped .
Use
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1)
to run it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name identifying the application, unique across the installation. It
doubles as the manager's module name and as the
.I x-euclid-target
requests address it by. Required.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-runtime " " \fIRUNTIME\fR
What the artifact is started with: \fIJAVA\fR (\fBjava \-jar\fR),
\fIPYTHON\fR (\fBpython3\fR), \fINODEJS\fR (\fBnode\fR) or \fIBINARY\fR
(the artifact itself, made executable). The interpreter is resolved
through \fIPATH\fR; Euclid launches processes and does not manage
language toolchains. Required.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fIBUCKET\fR
Name of the bucket holding the artifact. Required.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-artifact " " \fIKEY\fR
Object key of the artifact within that bucket, e.g.
\fIorders/orders-1.4.jar\fR. Required.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fR
Euclid user the application runs as. Omit it - the usual case - and the
application gets a technical principal of its own that cannot log in,
created here and deleted with it; see
.B IDENTITY
in
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1).
Name a user only when an application really should act as somebody who
already exists; that user must have an access key already.
.TP
.B \-\-buckets " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated names of the buckets the application may use. The
application's principal is granted exactly these and nothing else, so
the storage module refuses any other bucket it asks for. Omit it and the
principal keeps account-wide access.
.TP
.B \-\-queues " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated names of the queues the application may use, enforced
the same way by the queueing module.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Command to run instead of the runtime's default, for an application that
needs a wrapper script or an interpreter that is not on \fIPATH\fR. The
artifact path is passed to it as its first argument.
.TP
.B \-\-arguments " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated arguments passed after the artifact path.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-environment " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated \fIKEY=value\fR pairs set on the process, on top of the
\fIEUCLID_*\fR variables the manager injects - which these cannot
shadow.
.TP
.B \-\-min\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Smallest number of instances the autoscaler keeps running. Defaults to
1; there is no scale-to-zero.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Largest number of instances the autoscaler may scale out to. Defaults to
1, which pins the application to a single process.
.TP
.B \-\-ready\-timeout " " \fIMS\fR
How long an instance may take to create its socket before the manager
gives up on it and kills it, in milliseconds. Defaults to 30000. Worth
raising for a JVM or anything with a framework to load: an instance
killed for being slow to start is restarted, and starts slowly again.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application was defined. Its definition is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, an application of that name already exists, the runtime
is not one of the four, the bucket or the artifact does not exist, a
bucket or queue named by \fB\-\-buckets\fR/\fB\-\-queues\fR does not exist,
or a named user does not exist or has no access key.
.SH EXAMPLES
A Python application, scaling to four instances:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap create-application \e
    --application-id demo --runtime PYTHON \e
    --bucket apps --artifact euclid_app.py \e
    --min-instances 1 --max-instances 4
.fi
.RE
.PP
An application allowed to use one bucket and one queue, and nothing else:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap create-application \e
    --application-id inbox --runtime JAVA \e
    --bucket apps --artifact inbox.jar \e
    --buckets inbox --queues inbox-queue
.fi
.RE
.PP
A Java application with a heap setting and a longer start-up window:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap create-application \e
    --application-id inbox --runtime JAVA \e
    --bucket apps --artifact inbox.jar \e
    --environment JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS=-Xmx512m,INBOX_BUCKET=inbox \e
    --ready-timeout 45000
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
